   - Restrict to known IPs only via firewall/htaccess
   - Block system.multicall specifically:
     add_filter('xmlrpc_methods', function($methods) {
         unset($methods['system.multicall']);
         unset($methods['pingback.ping']);
         return $methods;
     });

Verify disabled:
curl -d '<methodCall><methodName>system.listMethods</methodName></methodCall>' https://yourdomain.com/xmlrpc.php
Should return 403 or 404

SEC-XMLRPC-002.remediation:
Block system.multicall and disable XML-RPC:

1. Quickest fix — disable entire XML-RPC:
   # .htaccess:
   <Files xmlrpc.php>
       Order Deny,Allow
       Deny from all
   </Files>

2. If XML-RPC must remain active, block multicall:
   add_filter('xmlrpc_methods', function($methods) {
       unset($methods['system.multicall']);
       unset($methods['system.listMethods']);
       unset($methods['system.getCapabilities']);
       return $methods;
   });

3. Enable Wordfence — it blocks XML-RPC brute force specifically:
   Wordfence > Firewall > Brute Force Protection
   Enable: 'Prevent WordPress from being discoverable via XML-RPC'

4. Rate limit XML-RPC at the web server level:
   # Nginx:
   location = /xmlrpc.php {
       limit_req zone=login burst=2 nodelay;
       limit_req_status 429;
   }

SEC-XMLRPC-003.remediation:
Disable pingback method while optionally keeping other XML-RPC methods:

// functions.php:
add_filter('xmlrpc_methods', function($methods) {
    unset($methods['pingback.ping']);
    unset($methods['pingback.extensions.getPingbacks']);
    return $methods;
});

// Or disable all of XML-RPC:
add_filter('xmlrpc_enabled', '__return_false');

Nginx — block entirely:
location = /xmlrpc.php {
    deny all;
    return 404;
}

Also block pingbacks at the WordPress level:
// Remove wp_head pingback link:
remove_action('wp_head', 'rsd_link');

WordPress Settings > Discussion > uncheck 'Allow link notifications from other blogs (pingbacks and trackbacks)'
